REPLACE_ME_WITH_A_LONG_RANDOM_PASSPHRASE

# Copy to secrets/restic-archive-password, replacing the line above with a
# real passphrase and deleting these comments -- restic reads the whole file.
#
# Generate one with:  openssl rand -base64 48
#
# Repository: rclone:yandex:restic/archive
#
# This one matters more than the home passphrase, not less. The archive holds
# cold data you have deleted locally, so its snapshots are the ONLY remaining
# copy. It is never forgotten and never pruned, by design -- a rolling
# retention policy would quietly garbage-collect the archive the moment its
# snapshot aged past the policy window, with no error and no warning.
#
# Because it is sole-copy data:
#   - keep this passphrase outside the repository (password manager, paper)
#   - run `restic-archive check` occasionally; metadata-only is cheap
#   - `restic-archive copy` to an external drive is the only thing in this
#     design that survives losing the Yandex account
#
# Ingest ritual -- the delete step is irreversible, so verify first:
#   restic-archive backup --tag archive ~/Local/some-old-thing
#   restic-archive ls latest --tag archive    # confirm it is actually there
#   ...only then remove the local copy
